SEQ 670 (R0): kwCompile, kjBuf and kjReq moved to the jitter's own incantation, incant/kantJitter, which this file
includes. ROW5 (R4): kwn's statement list holds a member that is no instruct (a bare 7). The interpreter's walk skips
it and so does the text: KW RAN kwn 2 of KW LEN 3. Before the skip the text called testOP on it, which refused.

kantJitWalkT -- SEQ 668 (Tony, 2026-11-16), the kant jitter's step 2c: the walk. kwCompile(action) walks the action's
finished chain in kant -- the BlocK's second slot is its statement list -- and writes one function with one testOP call
per top-level statement. The text reaches statement k by position (irAt, by name), asks irGoOn after each statement and
branches to the exit if not, and ends through irSeqEnd, which ends as the interpreter's statement walk does: a return's
value rides up, a break is caught and cleared. An if or a loop is one statement; its inside is not walked. The action
is then marked KanT, so ordinary calls run the walked function (step 2b's seat).
The three callers (kwrCall, kwbCall, kwfCall) are ordinary old-road actions, never compiled.
pop.sh runs this file twice: as written, and with the top-level kwCompile( and kwRan( lines deleted (the twin). Stdout
from KW S1 to KW END must be equal. KW S1 is newJitT's seven actions. ROW2: the statement after a return does not run
and 42 comes back. ROW3: a break at the top of an action ends it and does not reach the caller's loop. ROW4: the
statement after a refusal is skipped and the caller goes on. KW RAN is the text's own counter of statements run: for
the seven it equals KW LEN (every statement ran); kwr 2 of 3; kwb 4 over two calls (2 of 3 each); kwf 3 of 4. The text
between KW TEXT BEGIN and KW TEXT END is kwr's, pinned in pop.sh and compared across two runs (the second kwCompile(kwr)
only rewrites the text: the door answers REUSED).
